steps.body:
Add `else` after an `if` block to give the program a second path. When the condition is true the `if` block runs, otherwise the `else` block runs. Exactly one of the two runs, never both and never neither.

```cpp
if (n % 2 == 0) {
    std::cout << "even\n";
} else {
    std::cout << "odd\n";
}
```

Keep the braces on both blocks. Without them only the next single statement belongs to the branch, which is an easy way to run a line you meant to skip.

Odd or even is a natural two-way choice. Since `-7 % 2` is `-1`, not `1`, test even with `n % 2 == 0` and let `else` catch every odd number, negative ones included.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int n = 7;
    if (n % 2 == 0) {
        std::cout << n << " is even\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << n << " is odd\n";
    }

    const double balance = -20.5;
    if (balance < 0) {
        std::cout << "Overdrawn\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "In credit\n";
    }
    return 0;
}

