steps.body:
For three or more outcomes, chain conditions with `else if`. C++ checks them top to bottom and runs the block of the **first** true condition, then skips the rest of the chain. A final `else` with no condition is the catch-all for when nothing matched.

```cpp
if (score >= 90) {
    grade = 'A';
} else if (score >= 80) {
    grade = 'B';
} else {
    grade = 'F';
}
```

Because the first true condition wins, order matters. A score of 95 also satisfies `score >= 80`, but it never reaches that test because `score >= 90` already matched. Put the most specific (strictest) test first. If you flipped the order, every score of 80 or more would get a B.

The chain also means each test can be simple. Inside the `B` branch you already know the score is under 90, so you do not need `score >= 80 && score < 90`.

Do not replace `else if` with separate `if` statements. Each separate `if` is checked on its own, so a score of 95 would set `A` and then be overwritten with `B`.

To use the result after the chain, declare the variable **before** it, so it outlives the blocks.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int score = 83;
    char grade = '?';
    if (score >= 90) {
        grade = 'A';
    } else if (score >= 80) {
        grade = 'B';
    } else if (score >= 70) {
        grade = 'C';
    } else if (score >= 60) {
        grade = 'D';
    } else {
        grade = 'F';
    }
    std::cout << "Score " << score << ": " << grade << '\n';
    return 0;
}

