steps.body:
An `if` / `else` can sit inside another block. The inner test only runs when the outer branch was taken, which suits the leap year rule: divisible by 4, except centuries, except every 400 years.

```cpp
if (year % 4 == 0) {
    if (year % 100 == 0) {
        // a century: check 400
    } else {
        isLeap = true;
    }
}
```

Every pair of braces is a **block scope**. A variable declared inside one is created when that line runs and destroyed at its closing brace, so you cannot use it after the block. That is why `isLeap` is declared before the nested tests, starting at `false`.

An `else` belongs to the nearest `if` above it that has no `else` yet. Braces on every level make that pairing obvious.

C++17 adds `if (init; condition)`. The helper declared in the first part exists in the `if` **and** its `else`, then ends with the whole statement, so it stays out of the rest of `main`.

```cpp
if (int rem = 30 % 7; rem == 0) {
    std::cout << "Exact weeks\n";
} else {
    std::cout << rem << " days left over\n";
}
```

Ending a helper's scope early costs nothing at run time. It just stops the name from being used by mistake later in `main`.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int year = 1900;
    bool isLeap = false;
    if (year % 4 == 0) {
        if (year % 100 == 0) {
            if (year % 400 == 0) {
                isLeap = true;
            }
        } else {
            isLeap = true;
        }
    }

    if (isLeap) {
        std::cout << year << " is a leap year\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << year << " is not a leap year\n";
    }

    if (int rem = 30 % 7; rem == 0) {
        std::cout << "Exact weeks\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << rem << " days left over\n";
    }
    // rem no longer exists here
    return 0;
}

